# Every path has an owner; the entries below the catch-all exist so that a change to the release
# path or to an enforcement claim cannot be reviewed as if it were an ordinary change.
#
# CODEOWNERS only requests review — it does not block. It blocks when a branch protection rule on
# `main` turns on "Require review from Code Owners", which is a repository setting and therefore a
# HUMAN CHECKLIST item, not something this file can do on its own.

* @williamzujkowski

# The release path. A change here can publish something irreversible: npm's unpublish window is
# 72 hours, and the trusted-publisher configuration is bound to release.yml BY FILENAME.
/.github/workflows/release.yml          @williamzujkowski
/.github/workflows/release-please.yml   @williamzujkowski
/release-please-config.json             @williamzujkowski
/.release-please-manifest.json          @williamzujkowski
/scripts/check-release-versions.mjs     @williamzujkowski
/scripts/check-tarball-sources.mjs      @williamzujkowski
/scripts/assert-pnpm-pack.mjs           @williamzujkowski
/scripts/assert-pnpm-install.mjs        @williamzujkowski

# The claims. docs/threat-model.md is where capwall says what it does and does not stop; a diff
# here is a change to the product's honesty, not to its prose.
/docs/threat-model.md                   @williamzujkowski
/SECURITY.md                            @williamzujkowski

# The test gates that decide whether a security test can fail at all.
/scripts/mutants.json                   @williamzujkowski
/scripts/mutation-guard.mjs             @williamzujkowski
/scripts/mutation-sentinel.mjs          @williamzujkowski
